# Overview

This vignette follows `data-raw/tdc_add_estimate_year.r`, a complete example of adding one newly
released Texas Demographic Center estimate file to an existing population cube. It complements
`data-raw/tdc_estimates.r`, which creates the historical cube from all available source files.

Assume the existing cube contains estimates through 2023 and the file
`2024_ASRE_Estimate_alldata.csv` has been downloaded. `add_population_data()` reads only that file,
validates its dimensions against the existing cube, and writes an expanded cube containing 2024.

The update does not load the full historical cube into memory. Existing HDF5 data are copied in
blocks, the new year is written as a new slice, and the cube metadata are preserved. The source CSV
is necessarily read and transformed in memory, but the historical cube remains delayed.

# Set Up the Session

The example uses the packages, `paths` object, `county_fips`, and `county_names()` helper configured
by the first part of `data-raw/control_def.r`. In a package-development session, load that setup
before running the update script:

```{r setup}
library(tidyverse)
library(janitor)
library(data.table)
library(rage)
library(DelayedArray)

devtools::load_all()
paths <- tarr::paths

county_names <- function(x) {
  to_title <- purrr::compose(stringr::str_to_lower, stringr::str_to_title)
  x <- to_title(x)
  county_lookup <- rlang::set_names(names(county_fips), to_title(names(county_fips)))
  index <- match(x, names(county_lookup))
  x[!is.na(index)] <- county_lookup[index[!is.na(index)]]
  x
}
```

The script checks for `paths`, `county_fips`, and `county_names()` before doing any work. It also
stops if the existing cube cannot be found, because adding a year is different from creating a new
cube.

# Select the New Year and Source File

The year is the only value that normally changes between annual runs:

```{r update-year}
update_year <- 2024L

input_dir <- file.path(
  paths$population,
  "Estimates",
  "Texas Demographic Center",
  "asre"
)
update_pattern <- sprintf("^%d_ASRE_Estimate_alldata\\.csv$", update_year)
```

Anchoring the regular expression with `^` and `$` ensures that the reader selects exactly the
intended annual file. `read_tdc_estimate_year()` requires exactly one match and reports an error if
the file is missing or duplicated.

# Normalize Source Labels

Two small helpers keep age labels compatible with the historical cube. `process_tdc_update_age()`
removes source wording such as `"Ages"` and `"years"` and normalizes open-ended labels.
`ordered_tdc_update_ages()` converts the cleaned labels to age intervals before sorting them.

```{r age-helpers}
process_tdc_update_age <- function(x) {
  x |>
    stringr::str_remove_all(stringr::regex("Ages", ignore_case = TRUE)) |>
    stringr::str_remove_all(stringr::regex(" (ye?a??rs?|Ages)", ignore_case = TRUE)) |>
    stringr::str_trim(side = "both") |>
    stringr::str_replace("5\\+", "5 +")
}

ordered_tdc_update_ages <- function(x) {
  age_levels <- setdiff(levels(x), "All")
  c(sort(as.character(rage::as.age_group(age_levels))), "All")
}
```

`read_tdc_update_csv()` reads the selected file, cleans its column names, derives the year from its
file name, and harmonizes race-column terminology with the historical ingestion workflow.

```{r read-update-csv}
read_tdc_update_csv <- function(file, col_types) {
  readr::read_csv(
    file = file,
    col_types = col_types,
    id = "file_name",
    progress = TRUE
  ) |>
    janitor::clean_names() |>
    dplyr::mutate(
      year = basename(file_name) |>
        stringr::str_extract("^20[1-2][0-9]") |>
        as.integer()
    ) |>
    dplyr::select(-file_name) |>
    dplyr::rename_with(\(col) {
      col |>
        stringr::str_replace("anglo", "white") |>
        stringr::str_remove("^nh_")
    })
}
```

# Read One Annual File

The annual reader uses explicit source column types. County, FIPS, and age labels describe
dimensions, while the demographic columns contain integer population counts.

```{r annual-reader}
read_tdc_estimate_year <- function(...) {
  files <- list.files(
    path = input_dir,
    pattern = update_pattern,
    full.names = TRUE
  )

  if (length(files) != 1L) {
    cli::cli_abort(c(
      "Expected exactly one TDC estimate file for {update_year}.",
      "i" = "Matched {length(files)} files in {.file {input_dir}}."
    ))
  }

  col_types <- readr::cols(
    County = readr::col_factor(),
    FIPS = readr::col_factor(),
    Age = readr::col_factor(ordered = TRUE),
    .default = readr::col_integer()
  )

  read_tdc_update_csv(files[[1L]], col_types = col_types) |>
    data.table::setDT()
}
```

`read_tdc_update_csv()` performs the same source cleanup as the historical creation script. It
cleans column names, extracts `year` from the file name, and harmonizes older race-column names.
Keeping the annual and historical transformations equivalent is essential: an appended slice must
use the same labels as the cube it extends.

# Transform to the Cube Contract

`transform_tdc_estimate_year()` converts the wide source file into one row per population cell. Its
result has five dimension columns plus the value column:

```{r dimensions}
dims <- c("year", "area.name", "sex", "age.char", "race.eth")
```

The transformer in `data-raw/tdc_add_estimate_year.r`:

1. Normalizes wide source column names and melts demographic columns into long form.
2. Splits combined source labels into `race.eth` and `sex`.
3. Normalizes county and age labels to match the historical cube.
4. Removes the Texas total from the county partition.
5. Removes unavailable population values and physical `"All"` aggregate records.
6. Drops unused factor levels and restores canonical column order.

Age labels retain interval order through `rage::as.age_group()` and an ordered factor. This avoids
alphabetic ordering such as placing age 10 before age 2. Physical `"All"` rows are removed because
`tarr.pop` treats totals as virtual aggregates rather than independent cube cells.

The update transformer deliberately mirrors `transform_tdc_estimates()` in the cube-creation
script. If TDC changes its source layout, both workflows must be reviewed together so a new annual
slice cannot introduce labels that differ only because cleanup rules drifted.

# Locate the Existing Cube

The script uses the configured cube store, not the package installation directory:

```{r cube-path}
cube_root <- tarr.pop::init_cubes()
tdc_estimates_file <- file.path(cube_root, "base", "tdc_estimates_county.h5")
```

The historical cube must already exist at this location. It can be created with
`data-raw/tdc_estimates.r` as described in the Creating a Population Cube vignette.

# Add the New Year

The complete update call is:

```{r append-year}
default_counties <- setdiff(names(county_fips), "Texas")

tarr.pop::add_population_data(
  cube = tdc_estimates_file,
  reader = read_tdc_estimate_year,
  transformer = transform_tdc_estimate_year,
  dims = dims,
  add_dim = "year",
  completion_policy = "error",
  drop_all = TRUE,
  source_meta = list(
    note = sprintf(
      "Texas Demographic Center county estimates, updated through %d",
      update_year
    ),
    population_type = "Estimate",
    source = "Texas Demographic Center, Estimates program"
  ),
  data_col = "population",
  counties = default_counties,
  include_texas_total = FALSE
)
```

`cube` identifies the existing HDF5 file. `reader` and `transformer` supply only the incoming year.
`dims` must exactly match the existing cube's dimension order, and `add_dim = "year"` declares the
dimension being extended.

The non-year labels in the incoming data must already exist in the cube. If TDC introduces a new
race, age, sex, or area label, the function stops rather than silently changing the cube schema.
That change requires an explicit schema decision and may require a full rebuild.

With `completion_policy = "error"`, every expected cell for the new year must be present. This is
the safest default for a current annual release. Use `"na"` only when a missing combination is a
known source limitation, and use `"zero"` only when absence truly means a population of zero.

# How the Existing File Is Replaced

When `output_filepath` is omitted, `add_population_data()` writes a temporary expanded HDF5 file in
the same directory. After the write succeeds, it replaces the original cube and clears the package
cache. A failed validation occurs before replacement.

For a trial run, preserve the original cube by supplying another path:

```{r trial-output}
trial_file <- file.path(cube_root, "base", "tdc_estimates_county_with_2024.h5")

tarr.pop::add_population_data(
  cube = tdc_estimates_file,
  reader = read_tdc_estimate_year,
  transformer = transform_tdc_estimate_year,
  dims = dims,
  add_dim = "year",
  output_filepath = trial_file,
  overwrite = TRUE,
  completion_policy = "error",
  counties = default_counties,
  include_texas_total = FALSE
)
```

# Verify the Update

Reopening the cube and reading its dimension labels is metadata-oriented and does not realize all
population values:

```{r verify}
updated <- tarr.pop::open_poparray("tdc_estimates_county")

tarr.pop::years(updated)
stopifnot(as.character(update_year) %in% tarr.pop::years(updated))
tarr.pop::dim_semantics(updated)
tarr.pop::get_source(updated)
```

If value-level inspection is needed, subset first. Converting even a subset to a data frame is an
**EAGER** operation, so do not convert the full cube:

```{r inspect-slice}
check_2024 <- updated["2024", "Tarrant", , , , drop = FALSE]
head(as.data.frame(check_2024))
```

A tidyverse inspection is also **EAGER** after `as.data.frame()`:

```{r tidy-inspect}
updated["2024", "Tarrant", , , , drop = FALSE] |>
  as.data.frame() |>
  dplyr::slice_head(n = 10)
```

# Safeguards

`add_population_data()` refuses to append a year already present in the cube. It also validates the
incoming table, checks non-appended dimension labels, applies the completion policy, and preserves
the existing roles and dimension semantics. These checks prevent accidental duplication, silent
schema drift, and unsafe replacement of the historical cube.

Run the complete example after downloading the annual file with:

```{r run-script}
source("data-raw/tdc_add_estimate_year.r")
```
